<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Faraz Ahmed | Embodied AI Engineer</title><link>https://fxrarz.github.io/</link><atom:link href="https://fxrarz.github.io/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><description>Faraz Ahmed | Embodied AI Engineer</description><generator>Hugo Blox Builder (https://hugoblox.com)</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Mon, 24 Oct 2022 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><image><url>https://fxrarz.github.io/media/icon_hu_b7a7896a85bfabd7.png</url><title>Faraz Ahmed | Embodied AI Engineer</title><link>https://fxrarz.github.io/</link></image><item><title>Ardupilot SITL</title><link>https://fxrarz.github.io/blog/ardupilot/</link><pubDate>Fri, 03 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://fxrarz.github.io/blog/ardupilot/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Ardupilot is a fight controller (firmware) which controls and maintain the stability of the flight by taking commands. Here ardupilot software is used to control a drone in simulation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Various operation modes and waypilot following were implemented. See the below video.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="ardupilot-sitl"&gt;Ardupilot SITL&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div style="position: relative; padding-bottom: 56.25%; height: 0; overflow: hidden;"&gt;
&lt;iframe allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share; fullscreen" loading="eager" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/v=Np2wjhahrHc?autoplay=0&amp;amp;controls=1&amp;amp;end=0&amp;amp;loop=0&amp;amp;mute=0&amp;amp;start=0" style="position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; border:0;" title="YouTube video"&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</description></item><item><title>IsaacLab - DRL Workflow</title><link>https://fxrarz.github.io/blog/isaacsim/</link><pubDate>Fri, 03 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://fxrarz.github.io/blog/isaacsim/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Train DRL policy using IsaacLab has two approaches (workflow). 1st Direct based (fast code edits), 2nd is Manager-based (Reusable).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Both workflows yield same result. But different use-case.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Direct-Based workflow is like a whitebox. Entire Simulation, scene, terrain and training code are all under one roof. Easy to bring, modify and log things making fast development.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In Manager-based, everything is managed by different python and .yaml config files prioritizing code reusability. But, will be challenging chasing the file hierarchy for making any tweaks in the scene.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My take will be Manager-based workflow would be suitable for industry setting and Direct-based for researchers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thanks for Reading.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Pandas - Appending Cost</title><link>https://fxrarz.github.io/blog/pandas/</link><pubDate>Fri, 03 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://fxrarz.github.io/blog/pandas/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;For Robotics and Data Science projects i use Pandas for data collect and storage. Especially, for collecting live data from simulators for Behavior Cloning (BC), Pandas is handy for its consistency.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;During data collection, each data from simulator can be either appended to a existing list or dataframe which will be saved at the end. For multiple sensor data Pandas will be prefered over list due to multi-column insertion.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;# Code simulating contiguous memory
import pandas as pd
import time
num_cols = 5000 # Increasing this makes the &amp;quot;copy&amp;quot; operation much heavier
df = pd.DataFrame(columns=[f'c{i}' for i in range(num_cols)])
# This loop slows down gradually
for i in range(2500):
start = time.time()
df.loc[len(df)] = [1.1] * num_cols
print(f&amp;quot;Row {i}: {time.time() - start}s&amp;quot;)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Well everything might look good unless the data collection loop runs for 2000 iterations or more. Yes. That&amp;rsquo;s the breaking point. Technically, nothing is broken. Just your computer slows down (1 row insertion takes 3 seconds or more and increases) which is alot.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Its not your computer limitation or problem will Python. Just wrong function selection for the use case. We are dealing will DataFrame Fragmentation problem or Contiguous memory.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To understand this better, you need to know how computers store data. Contiguous memory means data is stored in one single, unbroken block in the computer&amp;rsquo;s RAM (like a row of houses with no gaps).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Pandas uses this method because it makes reading data very fast. However, it makes adding data very slow.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In my case, when you i use .loc to add a new row to a DataFrame, Pandas cannot simply &amp;ldquo;attach&amp;rdquo; the data to the end of the existing block. Usually, the space next to that block is already being used by other data.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To keep the memory contiguous, Pandas must:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;Find a new, empty area of memory that is large enough for the entire new DataFrame.
Copy every single old row into this new area.
Add the new row at the end.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As your loop runs, the DataFrame gets bigger. This means the computer spends more and more time copying data. If you have N rows, the time it takes grows at a rate of N² (Quadratic Time). This is why the loop over 2,000 iteration or more will be much slower.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The slow down time increases gradually. But, no issues with 300 or 500 steps. Beyond that you can watch a movie.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The best way to solve this issue is to avoid using a DataFrame until the very end. Instead, use a Python List.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Python Lists are designed to grow. They do not require a full copy of the data every time a new data item is added. Adding an item to a list is a Constant Time operation (O(1)), meaning it stays fast no matter how big the list is.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Then do Batch Construction. All collected in the list first will be converted as DataFrame once. This way i have reduced the data collection time.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;# Solution for contiguous memory
import pandas as pd
import time
num_cols = 5000 # Increasing this makes the &amp;quot;copy&amp;quot; operation much heavier
data_buffer = [] # list to store data
# This loop stays fast from start to finish
for i in range(2500):
start = time.time()
# Appending to a list is O(1)
data_buffer.append([1.1] * num_cols)
print(f&amp;quot;Row {i}: {time.time() - start}s&amp;quot;)
# Creating the DataFrame at the end is O(N)
df = pd.DataFrame(data_buffer, columns=[f'c{i}' for i in range(num_cols)])
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hope will be helpful for someone like me. Learned this hard way.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thanks for Reading.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Traverse Mechanism</title><link>https://fxrarz.github.io/projects/traverse_mechanism/</link><pubDate>Thu, 26 Oct 2023 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://fxrarz.github.io/projects/traverse_mechanism/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;A powerful traverse mechanism designed to move a 5 kg payload with high precision of up to 0.1 mm.&lt;/p&gt;
